{"qid":"Q12560","names":["Ottoman Empire"],"title":"Ottoman Empire","endonym":{"native":"دولت عالیه عثمانیه","lang":"ota"},"capsule":"The Ottoman Empire, emerging from a small Anatolian emirate under Osman I in the early 14th century, became one of the most powerful states in the world after Mehmed II captured Constantinople in 1453 and Selim I defeated the Safavids at Chaldiran (1514) and conquered Egypt (1517), making the sultan also caliph. At its height under Suleiman the Magnificent (r. 1520–1566) it controlled Anatolia, the Arab Middle East, Egypt, the Balkans, and Hungary, with its navy dominant in the eastern Mediterranean. The late 17th century brought reversal: the failed siege of Vienna (1683) opened a long Habsburg counter-offensive, and 19th-century nationalist movements dismembered the Balkans. Defeat in World War I led to the Armistice of Mudros (1918), Allied occupation, and the Turkish War of Independence; the sultanate was abolished in 1922 and the caliphate in 1924, ending the empire.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Administrative territories are well documented through Ottoman cadastral surveys (tapu tahrir defteri) and provincial records; Balkan and Arab provincial boundaries are known to high precision; frontier zones with Persia and in Arabia carry moderate uncertainty; overall confidence is high.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Ottoman Empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Ottoman-Empire"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Ottoman Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ottoman_Empire"}],"britannica":"place/Ottoman-Empire","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Ottoman Em